---
// web/src/pages/how-to-transfer-spotify-to-youtube-music.astro: the long-tail "how do I do this" guide.
// Targets the informational query; the landing page targets the transactional one, so the H1s stay distinct.
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
const title = 'How to transfer Spotify to YouTube Music · Sideload';
const description =
  'Move Spotify playlists, liked songs, albums and followed artists to YouTube Music for free, up to 25,000 songs a run. What moves, what does not, and how long it takes.';
const faq: [string, string][] = [
  [
    'Can I transfer Spotify to YouTube Music for free?',
    'Yes, and there is no paid tier to upgrade to: it runs on one small Cloudflare Worker that costs a few dollars a month. One run moves up to 25,000 songs, where most other transfer tools stop free users at a few hundred per transfer.',
  ],
  [
    'How many songs can I move at once?',
    "Up to 25,000 songs, 180 playlists, 2,000 saved albums and 2,000 followed artists in one run. The playlist number is YouTube's: it only lets any app create about 200 a day. A bigger library goes across in two runs, at no charge either time.",
  ],
  [
    'Do I need Spotify Premium?',
    'To create the Spotify app that Sideload connects to, yes. Since February 2026 Spotify caps every new app at 5 users, so a single shared login would break after the fifth person. Creating your own takes about two minutes and Sideload never sees a client secret.',
  ],
  [
    'Does it move liked songs and albums too?',
    'Yes: liked songs, saved albums and followed artists, not just playlists. Liked songs are a special case, see the <a href="https://sideload.marctorrelles.com/spotify-liked-songs-to-youtube-music">liked songs guide</a>.',
  ],
  [
    'Can I close the tab while it runs?',
    'Yes. The transfer runs on the server and survives crashes and restarts. The link to your transfer keeps working for 7 days, then the record is deleted.',
  ],
];
const jsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'FAQPage',
  mainEntity: faq.map(([q, a]) => ({ '@type': 'Question', name: q, acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: a } })),
};
---

<Base title={title} description={description} jsonLd={jsonLd}>
  <section class="container page">
    <p class="eyebrow page__eyebrow">Guide</p>
    <h1 class="h2">How to transfer Spotify playlists to YouTube Music</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Free, up to 25,000 songs a run, with no account and no app to install. It takes about two minutes to set up and
      its own while you do something else.
    </p>
    <p class="meta">Updated 7 September 2026.</p>

    <dl class="faq prose">
      <div class="faq__item">
        <dt>Step 1 · Connect Spotify and YouTube Music</dt>
        <dd>
          <p>
            Spotify connects with read-only access to your library, playlists and followed artists, plus your email
            address so the app you are signed in as is visible. You create the Spotify app it connects to, which takes
            about two minutes and needs a Premium account: Spotify caps every new app at 5 users, so a shared button
            would stop working after the fifth person. The Connect step walks you through it and never asks for a client
            secret.
          </p>
          <p>
            YouTube Music has no public API, so the sign-in is the one televisions use: Sideload shows a short code, you
            approve it once on <code>google.com/device</code> with your Google account, and that is it.
          </p>
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div class="faq__item">
        <dt>Step 2 · Pick what is worth keeping</dt>
        <dd>
          <p>
            Every playlist, your liked songs, your saved albums and your followed artists show up in one list with
            counts. Tick what you want. Ten years of a library usually contains a few years of things the algorithm made
            for you, and this is the moment to leave them behind. Playlists other people made that you follow are shown
            greyed out, because a personal Spotify app is not allowed to read them.
          </p>
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div class="faq__item">
        <dt>Step 3 · Close the tab</dt>
        <dd>
          <p>
            The transfer runs on the server, not in your browser, so it survives a closed laptop, a dropped connection
            and a crash. You get a link you can come back to. When it finishes you get a report: every song, where it
            landed, and the handful that need a decision from you.
          </p>
        </dd>
      </div>
    </dl>

    <h2 class="h3 sec">What moves, and what does not</h2>
    <table class="grid">
      <tbody>
        <tr><th scope="row">Playlists</th><td>Names, song order and the public or private setting are all kept.</td></tr
        >
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Liked songs</th>
          <td>Liked on YouTube Music, plus a private "Liked Songs" playlist that keeps your original order.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Saved albums</th><td>Saved to your YouTube Music library.</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Followed artists</th><td>Subscribed on YouTube Music.</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Podcasts</th><td>Not moved and not listed. YouTube Music handles shows differently.</td></tr
        >
        <tr
          ><th scope="row">Local files</th><td>Not moved, and listed in your report so nothing disappears quietly.</td
          ></tr
        >
        <tr><th scope="row">Playlist cover art</th><td>Not moved.</td></tr>
        <tr
          ><th scope="row">Followed playlists</th><td
            >Skipped: a personal Spotify app cannot read playlists owned by other people.</td
          ></tr
        >
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2 class="h3 sec">How long it takes</h2>
    <p class="page__more">
      About 45 songs a minute for songs nobody has moved before, and faster for songs already in the shared match cache.
      A 3,000 song library is usually under an hour. A few hundred songs is closer to ten minutes. Nothing is waiting on
      you while it runs.
    </p>

    <h2 class="h3 sec">The two parts most transfer tools get wrong</h2>
    <dl class="faq prose">
      <div class="faq__item">
        <dt>Songs that do not match are handed back, not dropped</dt>
        <dd>
          <p>
            Most songs match on the first try. The rest land in a "Needs your review" list with the closest candidate:
            accept it, search manually, or skip. Everything ends up in the report either way. Two cases are sent there
            on purpose: a song whose best match is a video another of your songs already used (a remix collapsing onto
            the studio version, which YouTube would silently merge), and a song YouTube accepted but never actually
            added.
          </p>
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div class="faq__item">
        <dt>Every playlist is read back after it is written</dt>
        <dd>
          <p>
            YouTube sometimes answers yes and does nothing. In the manual migration this tool grew out of, 346 of 2,585
            likes came back as success and never took effect. Sideload reads each playlist back after writing it and
            re-adds whatever is missing, up to three passes, which is why each playlist says "checking" at the end.
            Anything still missing after that lands in your review list rather than being called a success.
          </p>
        </dd>
      </div>
    </dl>

    <h2 class="h3 sec">What it costs</h2>
    <p class="page__more">
      Nothing, and no paywall halfway through. Sideload is <a
        href="https://github.com/marctorrelles/sideload"
        rel="noopener">MIT licensed and open source</a
      >, so you can also run your own copy. If you want to compare free tiers, see <a href="/alternatives"
        >how it stacks up against TuneMyMusic, Soundiiz and FreeYourMusic</a
      >. Your Spotify token goes the moment the transfer finishes, your YouTube token within 24 hours so you can fix
      unmatched songs first, and the transfer record 7 days later: the full detail is in <a href="/privacy"
        >privacy and terms</a
      >.
    </p>

    <h2 class="h3 sec">Common questions</h2>
    <dl class="faq">
      {
        faq.map(([q, a]) => (
          <div class="faq__item">
            <>
              <dt>{q}</dt>
              <dd set:html={a} />
            </>
          </div>
        ))
      }
    </dl>

    <a class="btn page__cta" href="/connect">Start a transfer</a>
  </section>
</Base>
<style>
  .prose p {
    margin: 0 0 12px;
  }
  .prose p:last-child {
    margin-bottom: 0;
  }
  .sec {
    margin: 46px 0 0;
  }
  .grid {
    border-collapse: collapse;
    width: 100%;
    max-width: 72ch;
    margin-top: 18px;
    font: 400 16px/1.5 var(--font-ui);
  }
  .grid th,
  .grid td {
    text-align: left;
    vertical-align: top;
    padding: 10px 12px 10px 0;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--hairline);
  }
  .grid th {
    color: var(--fg);
    font-weight: 500;
    white-space: nowrap;
    width: 11em;
  }
  .grid td {
    color: var(--fg-2);
  }
  .page__cta {
    margin-top: 40px;
  }
  @media (max-width: 900px) {
    .grid,
    .grid tbody,
    .grid tr,
    .grid th,
    .grid td {
      display: block;
      width: auto;
    }
    .grid th {
      white-space: normal;
    }
    .grid td {
      padding-top: 0;
      border-top: 0;
    }
  }
</style>
